const e=[{id:"react-comp-001",topic:"Components",subtopic:"Defining Components",difficulty:"easy",question:"What is a React component, and what are the minimal requirements for defining one?",answer:"A reusable UI element: a JavaScript function whose name starts with a capital letter and that returns JSX markup.",explanation:'```js\nexport default function Profile() {\n  return <img src="katherine.jpg" alt="Katherine Johnson" />;\n}\n```\nThat\'s the whole recipe — a plain function returning markup. `export default` makes it importable from other files. The capital letter isn\'t a style preference; it\'s how React distinguishes components from HTML tags.',tags:["components"]},{id:"react-comp-mcq-001",type:"multiple-choice",topic:"Components",subtopic:"Defining Components",difficulty:"easy",question:"You define `function profile() { … }` (lowercase) and render `<profile />`. What happens?",options:["React treats it as an unknown HTML tag instead of your component","It works — capitalization is only a naming convention","React throws a compile error naming the component","The component renders but cannot receive props"],answer:"React treats it as an unknown HTML tag instead of your component",explanation:"In JSX, lowercase tags (`<section>`, `<img>`) are HTML elements; capitalized tags (`<Profile />`) are components. A lowercase `<profile />` is passed to the DOM as a literal (unknown) HTML tag, and your function is never called. Component names must start with a capital letter.",tags:["components","jsx","mcq"]},{id:"react-comp-002",topic:"Components",subtopic:"Defining Components",difficulty:"hard",question:`Why must you never define a component inside another component?
\`\`\`js
export default function Gallery() {
  function Profile() {
    return <img src="..." />;
  }
  return <Profile />;
}
\`\`\``,answer:"Every render of `Gallery` creates a brand-new `Profile` function. React sees a different component each time, which causes performance problems and bugs — including state being reset on every parent render. Define all components at the top level of the file.",explanation:`\`\`\`js
function Profile() {
  return <img src="..." />;
}

export default function Gallery() {
  return <Profile />;
}
\`\`\`
React identifies components by function identity. A nested definition gets a new identity per render, so React unmounts and remounts it — discarding its state and DOM. Moving the definition out makes the identity stable.`,tags:["components","pitfalls"]},{id:"react-comp-003",topic:"Components",subtopic:"Defining Components",difficulty:"easy",question:"Why does multi-line JSX after `return` need parentheses?",answer:"Without parentheses, JavaScript's automatic semicolon insertion ends the statement at `return`, and everything after it is ignored.",explanation:'```js\nreturn (\n  <div>\n    <img src="..." alt="..." />\n  </div>\n);\n```\n`return` followed by a newline is read as `return;` — the function returns `undefined` and the JSX below is dead code. Parentheses keep the expression attached to the `return`.',tags:["components","jsx","pitfalls"]},{id:"react-jsx-001",topic:"JSX",subtopic:"Rules of JSX",difficulty:"easy",question:"What are the three rules of JSX?",answer:"1) Return a single root element. 2) Close every tag, including self-closing ones like `<img />`. 3) Write most attributes in camelCase (`className`, `strokeWidth`).",explanation:"JSX is stricter than HTML because it compiles to JavaScript: multiple roots would mean returning multiple objects from a function, unclosed tags are ambiguous, and attributes become JavaScript object keys — so `class` (a reserved word) becomes `className` and dashed names become camelCase.",tags:["jsx","rules"]},{id:"react-jsx-mcq-001",type:"multiple-choice",topic:"JSX",subtopic:"Rules of JSX",difficulty:"medium",question:"Why must JSX return a single root element?",options:["JSX compiles to JavaScript objects, and a function can't return two objects without wrapping them","Browsers can only attach one element at a time to the DOM","React's diffing algorithm only supports one tree per component","It is a stylistic rule enforced by the linter, not the language"],answer:"JSX compiles to JavaScript objects, and a function can't return two objects without wrapping them",explanation:`Each JSX tag becomes a JavaScript object. Two sibling tags at the top level would mean returning two objects from one function — invalid JavaScript without a wrapper. Wrap siblings in a parent tag or a Fragment:
\`\`\`js
return (
  <>
    <h1>Title</h1>
    <p>Content</p>
  </>
);
\`\`\``,tags:["jsx","fragments","mcq"]},{id:"react-jsx-002",topic:"JSX",subtopic:"Fragments",difficulty:"easy",question:"What is a Fragment (`<>...</>`), and how does it differ from wrapping siblings in a `<div>`?",answer:"A Fragment groups sibling JSX elements to satisfy the single-root rule, but unlike a `<div>` it leaves no trace in the rendered HTML.",explanation:"Wrapping in a `<div>` adds a real DOM node, which can break CSS layouts (flex/grid parents, lists) or produce invalid HTML (e.g. inside `<table>`). `<>...</>` is shorthand for `<Fragment>` and disappears at render time — grouping without markup.",tags:["jsx","fragments"]},{id:"react-jsx-mcq-002",type:"multiple-choice",topic:"JSX",subtopic:"Attributes",difficulty:"medium",question:"Most JSX attributes are camelCase (`className`, `strokeWidth`). Which attributes keep their HTML-style dashes?",options:["aria-* and data-* attributes","All SVG attributes","Event handlers like on-click","None — every attribute is camelCase in JSX"],answer:"aria-* and data-* attributes",explanation:'Attributes become JavaScript object keys, so `class` → `className` and `stroke-width` → `strokeWidth`. The historical exceptions are accessibility and data attributes, written exactly as in HTML:\n```js\n<div aria-label="description" data-id="123"></div>\n```',tags:["jsx","attributes","mcq"]},{id:"react-jsx-003",topic:"JSX",subtopic:"Curly Braces",difficulty:"medium",question:'In JSX, where do curly braces `{}` work, and what\'s wrong with `src="{avatar}"`?',answer:'Only in two places: as text inside tags (`<h1>{name}</h1>`) and as attribute values directly after `=` (`src={avatar}`). `src="{avatar}"` passes the literal string `"{avatar}"` — quotes turn it into plain text.',explanation:'```js\nconst avatar = \'https://example.com/photo.jpg\';\n\n<img src={avatar} alt="Scientist" />   // value of the variable\n<img src="{avatar}" alt="Scientist" />  // the 8-character string "{avatar}"\n```\nAny JavaScript expression works inside the braces, including function calls like `{formatDate(today)}` — but statements (`if`, `for`) do not, since JSX slots need values.',tags:["jsx","curly-braces"]},{id:"react-jsx-mcq-003",type:"multiple-choice",topic:"JSX",subtopic:"Curly Braces",difficulty:"medium",question:"What do the double curlies mean in `<ul style={{ backgroundColor: 'black' }}>`?",options:["A plain JavaScript object literal inside JSX's expression braces — nothing special","Special React syntax for inline styles only","A nested expression scope that re-evaluates on every render","An escaped curly brace, rendering a literal { character"],answer:"A plain JavaScript object literal inside JSX's expression braces — nothing special",explanation:"The outer braces are the usual JSX expression window; the inner braces are just an object literal. `style` happens to take an object, so you see `{{ }}` together. Gotcha: the object's CSS properties must be camelCase — `backgroundColor`, not `background-color`:\n```js\n<ul style={{ backgroundColor: 'black', color: 'pink' }}>\n```",tags:["jsx","curly-braces","style","mcq"]},{id:"react-jsx-004",topic:"JSX",subtopic:"Rules of JSX",difficulty:"medium",question:"Are JSX and React the same thing?",answer:"No. JSX is a syntax extension for writing markup in JavaScript; React is a UI library. They are designed to work together but can be used independently.",explanation:"Other libraries consume JSX (it compiles to ordinary function calls), and React can be used without JSX by calling `createElement` directly — just far less ergonomically. React pairs them because rendering logic and markup are inherently related, so components keep both in one place.",tags:["jsx","react"]},{id:"react-state-001",topic:"State",subtopic:"useState Basics",difficulty:"easy",question:`Why can't a regular local variable serve as a component's memory?
\`\`\`js
export default function Gallery() {
  let index = 0;
  function handleClick() {
    index = index + 1;
  }
}
\`\`\``,answer:"Two reasons: local variables reset on every render (React calls the function again from scratch), and changing one doesn't tell React to re-render — the screen never updates.",explanation:"`useState` fixes both problems at once: the state value *persists* between renders, and calling the setter *triggers* a re-render with the new value:\n```js\nconst [index, setIndex] = useState(0);\nfunction handleClick() {\n  setIndex(index + 1);\n}\n```",tags:["state","useState"]},{id:"react-state-002",topic:"State",subtopic:"useState Basics",difficulty:"easy",question:"What does `useState` return, and what does this line mean?\n```js\nconst [index, setIndex] = useState(0);\n```",answer:"It returns a two-item array: the current state value and a setter function. The line uses array destructuring; `0` is the initial value used on the first render.",explanation:"The naming is convention, not magic: `const [something, setSomething] = useState(initial)`. The setter both stores the new value and queues a re-render. On subsequent renders, `useState` ignores its argument and returns the stored value.",tags:["state","useState","destructuring"]},{id:"react-state-mcq-001",type:"multiple-choice",topic:"State",subtopic:"Rules of Hooks",difficulty:"medium",question:"Why can Hooks like `useState` only be called at the top level of a component — never inside conditions, loops, or nested functions?",options:["React matches each Hook to its stored state by a stable call order, which conditions would break","Hooks allocate memory that conditions would leak","JavaScript closures cannot capture variables declared inside if blocks","It is only a lint convention with no runtime consequence"],answer:"React matches each Hook to its stored state by a stable call order, which conditions would break",explanation:"React stores Hook state in order: first `useState` call → first slot, second call → second slot. If a Hook ran inside `if (condition)`, the call order would change between renders and every Hook after it would read the wrong slot. The rule keeps the order identical on every render.",tags:["state","rules-of-hooks","mcq"]},{id:"react-state-003",topic:"State",subtopic:"useState Basics",difficulty:"easy",question:`If you render the same component twice, do the instances share state?
\`\`\`js
export default function Page() {
  return (
    <>
      <Gallery />
      <Gallery />
    </>
  );
}
\`\`\``,answer:"No — state is isolated per component instance. Each `<Gallery />` has its own independent state, and clicking through one doesn't affect the other.",explanation:"State is also private to the component that declares it: the parent `Page` can neither read nor change a `Gallery`'s state. If two instances need to stay in sync, the state must be lifted up to the shared parent and passed down as props.",tags:["state","isolation"]},{id:"react-state-mcq-002",type:"multiple-choice",topic:"State",subtopic:"State as a Snapshot",difficulty:"medium",question:"Starting from `number = 0`, what does the next render show after this click?\n```js\n<button onClick={() => {\n  setNumber(number + 1);\n  setNumber(number + 1);\n  setNumber(number + 1);\n}}>+3</button>\n```",options:["1","3","0","2"],answer:"1",explanation:'State is a snapshot: within this render\'s handler, `number` is `0` in all three calls, so each one queues "replace with 1". Substituting makes it obvious:\n```js\nsetNumber(0 + 1);\nsetNumber(0 + 1);\nsetNumber(0 + 1);\n```\nTo actually increment three times, pass updater functions: `setNumber(n => n + 1)` three times.',tags:["state","snapshot","mcq"]},{id:"react-state-004",topic:"State",subtopic:"State as a Snapshot",difficulty:"hard",question:"`number` is 0. The user clicks, and the alert fires 3 seconds later — after the component re-rendered with `number = 5`. What does the alert show?\n```js\nfunction handleClick() {\n  setNumber(number + 5);\n  setTimeout(() => {\n    alert(number);\n  }, 3000);\n}\n```",answer:"0. The handler captured this render's snapshot of `number`, and that value never changes — not even inside async callbacks that run after the re-render.",explanation:'A state variable is fixed for the lifetime of the render that created it. The `setTimeout` closure holds the old snapshot — re-rendering creates *new* handlers with the new value, but it can\'t reach back into this one. This is why a delayed "send message" uses the recipient from the moment of the click, even if the user switches recipients during the delay.',tags:["state","snapshot","closures","async"]},{id:"react-state-005",topic:"State",subtopic:"Queueing Updates",difficulty:"medium",question:"What is an updater function, and when do you need one?\n```js\nsetNumber(n => n + 1);\n```",answer:"A function passed to the setter that computes the next state from the previous queued state. Use it when you need to update the same state multiple times before the next render, or whenever the new value depends on the old one.",explanation:"React queues updater functions and runs them in order during the next render, each receiving the previous one's result:\n```js\nsetNumber(n => n + 1); // 0 → 1\nsetNumber(n => n + 1); // 1 → 2\nsetNumber(n => n + 1); // 2 → 3\n```\nUnlike `setNumber(number + 1)`, which reads the frozen snapshot, `n` is always the latest pending value. Updaters must be pure — React may run them twice in Strict Mode to surface accidental side effects.",tags:["state","updater-functions"]},{id:"react-state-mcq-003",type:"multiple-choice",topic:"State",subtopic:"Queueing Updates",difficulty:"hard",question:"Starting from `number = 0`, what is the result after this handler runs?\n```js\nsetNumber(number + 5);\nsetNumber(n => n + 1);\nsetNumber(42);\n```",options:["42","6","48","5"],answer:"42",explanation:'React processes the queue in order: "replace with 5" → 5; updater `n => n + 1` applies to 5 → 6; then "replace with 42" discards everything before it → 42. A direct value replaces whatever is queued, while an updater transforms it — mixing them works, but the last direct set wins over everything prior.',tags:["state","updater-functions","batching","mcq"]},{id:"react-state-006",topic:"State",subtopic:"Queueing Updates",difficulty:"medium",question:'What does it mean that React "batches" state updates?',answer:"React waits until all code in an event handler has run before processing state updates and re-rendering. Multiple `set` calls in one handler produce a single re-render.",explanation:"Batching avoids wasteful intermediate renders — without it, three `set` calls would mean three renders, with the UI flashing through half-finished states. React does *not* batch across separate intentional events: two distinct clicks are processed independently, so a form can be disabled by the first click before the second is handled.",tags:["state","batching"]},{id:"react-state-007",topic:"State",subtopic:"State as a Snapshot",difficulty:"medium",question:'Explain the "substitution method" for reasoning about state in a render.',answer:"Mentally replace every occurrence of the state variable with its literal value for that render. The handler for a render where `number = 0` literally behaves as `setNumber(0 + 1)` — no live references involved.",explanation:"For the next render with `number = 1`, the same JSX produces a handler that reads `setNumber(1 + 1)`. Each render's props, handlers, and local variables are all computed from that render's snapshot — substitution makes async and event-handler behavior predictable instead of surprising.",tags:["state","snapshot","mental-model"]},{id:"react-effect-001",topic:"Effects & Lifecycle",subtopic:"What Effects Are",difficulty:"easy",question:"What is an Effect in React, and how does it differ from an event handler?",answer:"An Effect is a side effect caused by *rendering itself* — used to synchronize the component with an external system (server, browser API, third-party widget). Event handlers run in response to a specific user action; Effects run after render, regardless of which interaction made the component appear.",explanation:`\`\`\`js
useEffect(() => {
  const connection = createConnection();
  connection.connect();
}, []);
\`\`\`
A chat component must connect whenever it's on screen — not because of any particular click. That "because it rendered" logic belongs in an Effect. "Because the user did X" logic belongs in an event handler.`,tags:["effects","useEffect"]},{id:"react-effect-mcq-001",type:"multiple-choice",topic:"Effects & Lifecycle",subtopic:"Dependencies",difficulty:"medium",question:"What is the difference between these three Effects?\n```js\nuseEffect(() => { /* A */ });\nuseEffect(() => { /* B */ }, []);\nuseEffect(() => { /* C */ }, [isPlaying]);\n```",options:["A runs after every render, B only on mount, C on mount and whenever isPlaying changes","A runs once, B never runs, C runs only when isPlaying is true","All three are identical; the array is documentation only","A runs before render, B after render, C during render"],answer:"A runs after every render, B only on mount, C on mount and whenever isPlaying changes",explanation:"The dependency array controls when the Effect re-runs after a render: omitted → every render; empty `[]` → only when the component mounts; `[isPlaying]` → on mount and after any render where `isPlaying` changed (compared with `Object.is`). Dependencies aren't optional tuning — every reactive value the Effect reads must be listed.",tags:["effects","dependencies","mcq"]},{id:"react-effect-002",topic:"Effects & Lifecycle",subtopic:"Cleanup",difficulty:"medium",question:`What is an Effect cleanup function, and when does React call it?
\`\`\`js
useEffect(() => {
  const connection = createConnection();
  connection.connect();
  return () => connection.disconnect();
}, []);
\`\`\``,answer:"The function returned from an Effect. React calls it before the Effect runs again (when dependencies change) and once more when the component unmounts.",explanation:"Cleanup undoes whatever the Effect set up: disconnect connections, remove event listeners, cancel timers, stop animations. The mental model is symmetry — each Effect describes how to *start* synchronizing and how to *stop*, and React alternates them as needed.",tags:["effects","cleanup"]},{id:"react-effect-mcq-002",type:"multiple-choice",topic:"Effects & Lifecycle",subtopic:"Strict Mode",difficulty:"medium",question:"In development, your Effect with `[]` dependencies runs twice on mount, with a cleanup in between. Why?",options:["Strict Mode deliberately remounts components once in development to surface missing cleanup bugs","The component has a bug causing an extra render","The dependency array is being mutated between renders","React batches the first two renders and replays both Effects"],answer:"Strict Mode deliberately remounts components once in development to surface missing cleanup bugs",explanation:"The dev-only sequence is mount → effect → cleanup → effect. If your Effect has correct cleanup, the double pass is invisible; if something breaks (duplicate connections, double subscriptions), you've found a real bug that would appear in production navigation. Don't suppress it with a `useRef` mounted-flag — fix the cleanup. Production runs the Effect once.",tags:["effects","strict-mode","mcq"]},{id:"react-effect-003",topic:"Effects & Lifecycle",subtopic:"Fetching",difficulty:"hard",question:`What problem does the \`ignore\` flag solve in this data-fetching Effect?
\`\`\`js
useEffect(() => {
  let ignore = false;
  async function startFetching() {
    const json = await fetchTodos(userId);
    if (!ignore) setTodos(json);
  }
  startFetching();
  return () => { ignore = true; };
}, [userId]);
\`\`\``,answer:"Race conditions. If `userId` changes while a fetch is in flight, the old request's cleanup sets its `ignore` to true, so a slow stale response can't overwrite the newer one.",explanation:"Without the flag: switch from user A to user B, B's response arrives first, then A's slow response lands and clobbers the state — the UI shows the wrong user's todos. Each Effect run gets its own `ignore` variable; cleanup poisons the *previous* run before the next begins. The network request isn't cancelled, but its result is discarded.",tags:["effects","fetching","race-conditions"]},{id:"react-effect-004",topic:"Effects & Lifecycle",subtopic:"When Not to Use",difficulty:"medium",question:`What's wrong with this code, and what's the fix?
\`\`\`js
const [firstName, setFirstName] = useState('');
const [lastName, setLastName] = useState('');
const [fullName, setFullName] = useState('');

useEffect(() => {
  setFullName(firstName + ' ' + lastName);
}, [firstName, lastName]);
\`\`\``,answer:"`fullName` is derived data — it doesn't need state or an Effect at all. Calculate it during render:\n```js\nconst fullName = firstName + ' ' + lastName;\n```",explanation:"The Effect version renders with a stale `fullName`, then re-renders after the Effect sets it — an extra render pass and an extra state variable to keep in sync. If something can be computed from existing props/state, compute it during render. Effects are for synchronizing with systems *outside* React, not for transforming data inside it.",tags:["effects","derived-state","anti-patterns"]},{id:"react-effect-mcq-003",type:"multiple-choice",topic:"Effects & Lifecycle",subtopic:"When Not to Use",difficulty:"hard",question:"A POST request to `/api/buy` should fire when the user clicks Buy. Where does it belong?",options:["In the click's event handler — it's caused by a specific interaction, not by rendering","In a useEffect with empty deps, so it runs exactly once","In a useEffect depending on a `purchased` state flag set by the click","Directly in the component body, guarded by an if"],answer:"In the click's event handler — it's caused by a specific interaction, not by rendering",explanation:"```js\nfunction handleClick() {\n  fetch('/api/buy', { method: 'POST' });\n}\n```\nAn Effect with `[]` would buy the product because the page *rendered* — including on a dev remount, or whenever the component reappears. The flag-state variant is the same bug with extra steps. The question to ask: should this code run because the component was displayed, or because the user did something? Buying is the latter.",tags:["effects","event-handlers","anti-patterns","mcq"]},{id:"react-effect-005",topic:"Effects & Lifecycle",subtopic:"Subscriptions",difficulty:"medium",question:"Write an Effect that subscribes to window scroll events — including correct cleanup.",answer:`\`\`\`js
useEffect(() => {
  function handleScroll(e) {
    console.log(window.scrollX, window.scrollY);
  }
  window.addEventListener('scroll', handleScroll);
  return () => window.removeEventListener('scroll', handleScroll);
}, []);
\`\`\``,explanation:"The subscription pattern: add the listener in the Effect body, remove it in the cleanup, with the same function reference (which is why `handleScroll` is named, not inline twice). Without cleanup, every mount leaks a listener — and Strict Mode's dev remount makes the leak visible immediately as doubled logs.",tags:["effects","subscriptions","cleanup"]},{id:"react-effect-006",topic:"Effects & Lifecycle",subtopic:"What Effects Are",difficulty:"easy",question:"When does an Effect actually run, relative to rendering?",answer:"After the render is committed to the screen. React first updates the DOM, then runs Effects.",explanation:"That ordering is the point: by the time an Effect runs, the DOM reflects the latest render, so the Effect can safely read layout, attach widgets, or play a `<video>` element that just appeared. It's also why side effects are forbidden *during* render — render must stay a pure calculation.",tags:["effects","lifecycle","rendering"]},{id:"react-effect-007",topic:"Effects & Lifecycle",subtopic:"Lifecycle",difficulty:"medium",question:"From an Effect's point of view, what is the component lifecycle?",answer:"Just two operations, possibly repeated: *start synchronizing* (run the Effect) and *stop synchronizing* (run the cleanup). Mount runs the Effect; each dependency change runs cleanup then the Effect again; unmount runs the final cleanup.",explanation:'For a chat room Effect depending on `[roomId]`, navigating general → travel → music produces: connect(general), disconnect(general), connect(travel), disconnect(travel), connect(music), disconnect(music). Thinking in "sync/unsync cycles" rather than "mount/update/unmount" is the model react.dev teaches — each cycle is independent and self-contained.',tags:["effects","lifecycle","cleanup"]},{id:"react-props-001",topic:"Props & Composition",subtopic:"Passing Props",difficulty:"easy",question:"How do you pass props to a component, and how does the child read them?",answer:"Pass them like HTML attributes — `<Avatar person={obj} size={100} />` — and read them by destructuring the function's single argument: `function Avatar({ person, size }) { … }`.",explanation:"```js\n<Avatar person={{ name: 'Lin Lanying', imageId: '1bX5QH6' }} size={100} />\n\nfunction Avatar({ person, size }) {\n  return <img src={getImageUrl(person)} width={size} />;\n}\n```\nDestructuring is sugar for `function Avatar(props) { let person = props.person; … }`. Any JavaScript value can be a prop — objects, arrays, functions, even other JSX.",tags:["props"]},{id:"react-props-mcq-001",type:"multiple-choice",topic:"Props & Composition",subtopic:"Passing Props",difficulty:"medium",question:"Given `function Avatar({ size = 100 }) {}`, in which case does `size` NOT get the default 100?",options:["<Avatar size={null} />","<Avatar />","<Avatar size={undefined} />","All three use the default"],answer:"<Avatar size={null} />",explanation:"Destructuring defaults kick in only for *missing* or `undefined` values. Omitting the prop and passing `undefined` both trigger the default; `null` (and `0`) are real values that pass straight through — so `size` is `null` in the first case, which is usually a bug to watch for.",tags:["props","defaults","mcq"]},{id:"react-props-002",topic:"Props & Composition",subtopic:"Passing Props",difficulty:"medium",question:`What does spread syntax do in JSX, and when should you be cautious with it?
\`\`\`js
function Profile(props) {
  return (
    <div className="card">
      <Avatar {...props} />
    </div>
  );
}
\`\`\``,answer:"`{...props}` forwards every prop of `Profile` to `Avatar` without naming them. Use it with restraint — if many components just relay all their props, that often signals the structure should be refactored (e.g. pass `children` instead).",explanation:"Spread is handy for thin wrappers that add styling or layout around a child. But it hides the data flow: readers can't see what `Avatar` actually receives. react.dev's advice is that pervasive spreading is a smell, not a pattern to default to.",tags:["props","spread"]},{id:"react-props-003",topic:"Props & Composition",subtopic:"children",difficulty:"easy",question:"What is the `children` prop?\n```js\n<Card>\n  <Avatar />\n</Card>\n```",answer:"Whatever JSX is nested between a component's opening and closing tags arrives as its `children` prop — here, `Card` receives `<Avatar />` as `children`.",explanation:'```js\nfunction Card({ children }) {\n  return <div class="card">{children}</div>;\n}\n```\nThis is React\'s primary composition mechanism: `Card` provides the frame without knowing what\'s inside it. Panels, layouts, modals, and grids all use the children "hole" pattern.',tags:["props","children","composition"]},{id:"react-props-mcq-002",type:"multiple-choice",topic:"Props & Composition",subtopic:"Immutability",difficulty:"medium",question:"A child component wants to change a value it received as a prop. What is the React way?",options:["Ask the parent to pass different props — typically by calling an event-handler prop that sets the parent's state","Mutate the prop object directly; React will detect the change","Copy the prop into local state and mutate the copy on every render","Use a ref to override the prop value"],answer:"Ask the parent to pass different props — typically by calling an event-handler prop that sets the parent's state",explanation:"Props are immutable snapshots of the parent's data — a component can't (and must never) change its own props. When something needs to change in response to interaction, the owner of that data sets state, and React re-renders the child with *new* props. Copying props into state just creates two sources of truth that drift apart.",tags:["props","immutability","data-flow","mcq"]},{id:"react-list-001",topic:"Props & Composition",subtopic:"Rendering Lists",difficulty:"easy",question:"How do you render a list of items from an array in React?",answer:"Transform the data with `map()` into an array of JSX elements and embed it, giving each element a `key`:\n```js\n<ul>\n  {people.map(person =>\n    <li key={person.id}>{person.name}</li>\n  )}\n</ul>\n```",explanation:"JSX accepts arrays of elements, so `map` (often after a `filter`) is the standard pattern — there is no special list syntax. The `key` attribute on the outermost mapped element is required; React warns in the console when it's missing.",tags:["lists","map","keys"]},{id:"react-list-mcq-001",type:"multiple-choice",topic:"Props & Composition",subtopic:"Keys",difficulty:"medium",question:"Why does React need a `key` on list items?",options:["Keys let React match each item across renders, so insertions, deletions, and reorders keep the right component state","Keys are required to make list rendering compile","Keys are passed to the component so it knows its position","Keys improve SEO by labeling repeated elements"],answer:"Keys let React match each item across renders, so insertions, deletions, and reorders keep the right component state",explanation:'Without keys React falls back to position: delete the first item and every component shifts identity — the second item inherits the first\'s state (checkbox ticks, input text). A stable key says "this is still the same item even though it moved". Note keys are hints to React only; they are *not* passed as a prop — if the component needs the ID, pass it separately: `<Profile key={id} userId={id} />`.',tags:["keys","lists","mcq"]},{id:"react-list-002",topic:"Props & Composition",subtopic:"Keys",difficulty:"medium",question:"What are the rules for choosing keys, and where should they come from?",answer:"Keys must be unique among siblings and stable across renders. Use database IDs for server data; for locally created items, generate an ID once at creation time (incrementing counter, `crypto.randomUUID()`). Never generate keys during render.",explanation:"`key={Math.random()}` is the canonical mistake: every render produces new keys, so React destroys and recreates every item — all state lost, performance wrecked. Uniqueness is only required within one array; two different lists can reuse the same keys.",tags:["keys","lists"]},{id:"react-list-mcq-002",type:"multiple-choice",topic:"Props & Composition",subtopic:"Keys",difficulty:"hard",question:"A sortable todo list uses `key={index}`. The user types into the first todo's input, then sorts the list. What happens?",options:["The typed text stays in the first row even though a different todo is now there","The text follows its todo to the new position","React throws a duplicate-key error","Nothing — index keys behave identically to ID keys"],answer:"The typed text stays in the first row even though a different todo is now there",explanation:`With index keys, "item at position 0" is the same component identity before and after the sort — so its DOM/state (the input's text, focus) stays at position 0 while the underlying data moved elsewhere. Index keys are only safe for static lists that never reorder, filter, or insert. Use a stable per-item ID instead.`,tags:["keys","lists","pitfalls","mcq"]},{id:"react-props-004",topic:"Props & Composition",subtopic:"Immutability",difficulty:"medium",question:`How do props "change over time" if they're immutable?`,answer:"A component receives new props each render. The props themselves are never mutated — when the parent's state changes, it re-renders and passes a *different* props object to the child.",explanation:"```js\nfunction Clock({ color, time }) {\n  return <h1 style={{ color }}>{time}</h1>;\n}\n```\n`Clock` may receive a new `time` every second, but each render's props are a fixed snapshot. Think of props as a stream of immutable values, not a mutable binding — the same snapshot model as state.",tags:["props","immutability","rendering"]},{id:"react-form-001",topic:"Forms",subtopic:"Controlled Inputs",difficulty:"easy",question:"What's the difference between a controlled and an uncontrolled input?",answer:'Controlled: React owns the value via state — `<input value={value} onChange={e => setValue(e.target.value)} />`. Uncontrolled: the browser DOM owns the value; React only sets the initial one — `<input defaultValue="initial" />`.',explanation:"Controlled inputs let you re-render on every keystroke, validate live, or transform/limit input programmatically. Uncontrolled inputs are simpler when you only need the value at submit time (e.g. via `FormData`). The deciding prop is `value` (controls) vs `defaultValue` (doesn't).",tags:["forms","controlled-inputs"]},{id:"react-form-mcq-001",type:"multiple-choice",topic:"Forms",subtopic:"Controlled Inputs",difficulty:"medium",question:"What happens if you pass `value` to an input without an `onChange` handler?\n```js\n<input value={something} />\n```",options:["The input becomes read-only and React logs an error in the console","The input works normally, managed by the browser","The component fails to render","React automatically creates a state variable for it"],answer:"The input becomes read-only and React logs an error in the console",explanation:"A controlled input always displays exactly its `value` prop. The user types, the DOM asks to change, React re-renders with the same `value` — so nothing visibly happens. If you want it editable, add `onChange={e => setSomething(e.target.value)}`; if you want browser-managed, use `defaultValue` instead. Intentionally read-only inputs should say so: `readOnly`.",tags:["forms","controlled-inputs","mcq"]},{id:"react-form-002",topic:"Forms",subtopic:"Controlled Inputs",difficulty:"hard",question:'Why can this cause the warning "a component is changing an uncontrolled input to be controlled"?\n```js\n<input value={someValue} onChange={handleChange} />\n```',answer:"If `someValue` is `undefined` or `null` on some renders, the input flips between uncontrolled (no value) and controlled (string value). An input must stay one or the other for its whole lifetime. Fix: `value={someValue ?? ''}`.",explanation:"React decides controlled-vs-uncontrolled per render from the `value` prop. A state initialized as `useState()` (undefined) that later becomes a string triggers the switch — and with it, lost user input and the console warning. Always initialize text-input state to `''`, never `undefined`/`null`.",tags:["forms","controlled-inputs","pitfalls"]},{id:"react-form-mcq-002",type:"multiple-choice",topic:"Forms",subtopic:"Controlled Inputs",difficulty:"hard",question:"Why does this handler make the text caret jump to the end while typing?\n```js\nfunction handleChange(e) {\n  setFirstName(e.target.value.toUpperCase());\n}\n```",options:["The stored value differs from what the user typed, so React rewrites the input and the browser resets the caret","toUpperCase() is asynchronous, delaying the state update","Event objects are pooled, so e.target.value is stale by the time setState runs","Uppercase characters render wider, pushing the caret right"],answer:"The stored value differs from what the user typed, so React rewrites the input and the browser resets the caret",explanation:"A controlled input only preserves the caret when the value round-trips unchanged. Storing a *transformed* value (or updating asynchronously, e.g. inside `setTimeout`) means React must overwrite the DOM input with something other than what the user just typed — and overwriting resets caret position. Update state synchronously with the exact `e.target.value`, and transform for display elsewhere.",tags:["forms","controlled-inputs","pitfalls","mcq"]},{id:"react-form-003",topic:"Forms",subtopic:"Checkboxes",difficulty:"easy",question:"How does a controlled checkbox differ from a controlled text input?",answer:"Checkboxes use the `checked` prop (boolean) instead of `value`, and the handler reads `e.target.checked` instead of `e.target.value`.",explanation:'```js\nconst [isChecked, setIsChecked] = useState(false);\n\n<input\n  type="checkbox"\n  checked={isChecked}\n  onChange={e => setIsChecked(e.target.checked)}\n/>\n```\nPassing `value={isChecked}` to a checkbox is a classic bug — `value` on a checkbox is the form-submission value, not its checked state. The uncontrolled form is `defaultChecked={true}`.',tags:["forms","checkboxes"]},{id:"react-form-004",topic:"Forms",subtopic:"Declarative UI",difficulty:"medium",question:"What's the difference between imperative and declarative UI programming, per react.dev's taxi analogy?",answer:"Imperative: you issue step-by-step instructions for *how* to change the UI (enable this, hide that) — like giving a driver turn-by-turn directions. Declarative: you describe *what* the UI looks like for each state and React figures out the DOM updates — like telling a taxi the destination.",explanation:"Imperative manipulation (`disable()`, `show()`, `hide()` calls scattered through handlers) grows quadratically harder as states multiply — every new feature risks breaking the choreography. In React you declare the UI per state (`disabled={status === 'submitting'}`) and never touch the DOM directly.",tags:["forms","declarative","mental-model"]},{id:"react-form-005",topic:"Forms",subtopic:"Declarative UI",difficulty:"medium",question:"react.dev describes 5 steps for thinking about UI declaratively. What are they?",answer:"1) Identify the component's visual states (empty, typing, submitting, success, error). 2) Determine what triggers each transition (human inputs, computer inputs). 3) Represent the state with `useState`. 4) Remove non-essential state variables. 5) Connect event handlers to set the state.",explanation:"The form example lands on three variables:\n```js\nconst [answer, setAnswer] = useState('');\nconst [error, setError] = useState(null);\nconst [status, setStatus] = useState('typing'); // 'typing' | 'submitting' | 'success'\n```\nEverything else is derived: \"empty\" is `answer.length === 0`, \"isError\" is `error !== null`. The structure makes invalid combinations unrepresentable.",tags:["forms","declarative","state-design"]},{id:"react-form-mcq-003",type:"multiple-choice",topic:"Forms",subtopic:"State Design",difficulty:"hard",question:"A form tracks `isTyping`, `isSubmitting`, and `isSuccess` as three separate booleans. What's the design flaw?",options:["It allows impossible states, like isTyping and isSubmitting both true — one status variable rules them out","Booleans are slower to update than strings in React state","Three useState calls always cause three separate re-renders","Boolean state cannot be used in conditional rendering"],answer:"It allows impossible states, like isTyping and isSubmitting both true — one status variable rules them out",explanation:"Three booleans encode 8 combinations, of which most are paradoxes you must remember to prevent in every handler. A single `status: 'typing' | 'submitting' | 'success'` makes exactly the valid states expressible. The react.dev questions to ask of each state variable: does it create a paradox? Is it already available elsewhere? Can it be derived from another variable?",tags:["forms","state-design","mcq"]},{id:"react-form-006",topic:"Forms",subtopic:"Submission",difficulty:"medium",question:`Walk through what this submit handler does, including why it calls \`e.preventDefault()\`.
\`\`\`js
async function handleSubmit(e) {
  e.preventDefault();
  setStatus('submitting');
  try {
    await submitForm(answer);
    setStatus('success');
  } catch (err) {
    setStatus('typing');
    setError(err);
  }
}
\`\`\``,answer:"`preventDefault()` stops the browser's default form submission (full page reload). Then the handler drives the declarative state machine: enter `submitting` (UI disables itself), and on resolution move to `success` or back to `typing` with the error recorded.",explanation:"The handler never touches the DOM — it only sets state, and the JSX declares what each status looks like: `disabled={status === 'submitting'}` on the textarea, the button disabled when empty or submitting, the error shown when `error !== null`, and an early-return `<h1>` for success.",tags:["forms","submission","events"]},{id:"react-form-007",topic:"Forms",subtopic:"Controlled Inputs",difficulty:"easy",question:"In a controlled input's `onChange` handler, where does the new value come from?",answer:"From the event object: `e.target.value` — `e.target` is the DOM input element that fired the event.",explanation:"```js\n<input value={text} onChange={e => setText(e.target.value)} />\n```\nThe cycle: user types → browser fires the change event with the would-be value → handler stores it in state → React re-renders → the input displays the state. For checkboxes the equivalent is `e.target.checked`.",tags:["forms","events","controlled-inputs"]},{id:"react-ctx-001",topic:"Context",subtopic:"Prop Drilling",difficulty:"easy",question:"What problem does React Context solve?",answer:"Prop drilling — passing data through many intermediate components that don't use it, just to reach a deeply nested child. Context lets a parent make data available to *any* descendant directly, regardless of depth.",explanation:"Drilling a `theme` or `currentUser` prop through five layout components clutters every signature on the way down. With context, the components in between don't even know the data exists — it flows past them to whoever asks for it.",tags:["context","prop-drilling"]},{id:"react-ctx-002",topic:"Context",subtopic:"Using Context",difficulty:"medium",question:"What are the three steps to use context?",answer:"1) Create it: `export const LevelContext = createContext(1);` (argument = default value). 2) Read it where needed: `const level = useContext(LevelContext);`. 3) Provide it from a parent: wrap children in `<LevelContext value={level}>…</LevelContext>`.",explanation:`\`\`\`js
// LevelContext.js
export const LevelContext = createContext(1);

// Heading.js — consumes
const level = useContext(LevelContext);

// Section.js — provides
<LevelContext value={level}>
  {children}
</LevelContext>
\`\`\`
The context object usually lives in its own file so both sides can import it. Components between provider and consumer need no changes — the value flows through them.`,tags:["context","useContext","createContext"]},{id:"react-ctx-mcq-001",type:"multiple-choice",topic:"Context",subtopic:"Using Context",difficulty:"medium",question:"A component calls `useContext(ThemeContext)` and there are two `ThemeContext` providers above it in the tree with different values. Which value does it get?",options:["The nearest provider above it wins","The topmost (outermost) provider wins","The values are merged into one object","It's an error to nest providers of the same context"],answer:"The nearest provider above it wins",explanation:"Context lookup walks *up* the tree and stops at the closest matching provider — like CSS inheritance. Nesting providers is a feature: a component can read the value from above and provide a modified one below (`<LevelContext value={level + 1}>`), which is how auto-nesting headings work. With no provider at all, the consumer gets the default passed to `createContext`.",tags:["context","providers","mcq"]},{id:"react-ctx-003",topic:"Context",subtopic:"When to Use",difficulty:"medium",question:"What two alternatives does react.dev recommend trying before reaching for context?",answer:"1) Just pass props — explicit data flow is verbose but self-documenting. 2) Component composition: extract components and pass JSX as `children`, so intermediate layers stop relaying data they don't use — `<Layout><Posts posts={posts} /></Layout>` instead of `<Layout posts={posts} />`.",explanation:'Often "prop drilling" pain really means too many layers that don\'t render anything with the data. Restructuring with `children` removes the middlemen entirely. Context earns its place for genuinely tree-wide concerns: theming, current user, routing, and state shared with a reducer.',tags:["context","composition","best-practices"]},{id:"react-ctx-mcq-002",type:"multiple-choice",topic:"Context",subtopic:"Using Context",difficulty:"easy",question:"What does the argument to `createContext(1)` mean?",options:["The default value consumers get when no provider is above them in the tree","The maximum number of providers allowed","The initial value providers must pass on first render","A version number for the context"],answer:"The default value consumers get when no provider is above them in the tree",explanation:"`createContext(defaultValue)` sets the fallback for consumers rendered outside any provider — useful for tests, storybook isolation, or top-level components. Once any matching provider wraps the consumer, the provider's `value` always takes precedence.",tags:["context","createContext","mcq"]},{id:"react-ref-001",topic:"Refs",subtopic:"useRef Basics",difficulty:"easy",question:"What does `useRef(0)` return, and what are its two defining behaviors?",answer:"A plain object `{ current: 0 }`. It persists between renders (React keeps the same object), and mutating `ref.current` does *not* trigger a re-render.",explanation:`\`\`\`js
const ref = useRef(0);

function handleClick() {
  ref.current = ref.current + 1;
  alert('Clicked ' + ref.current + ' times!'); // no re-render
}
\`\`\`
Think of a ref as a per-instance pocket React doesn't look inside: stable storage that's invisible to rendering.`,tags:["refs","useRef"]},{id:"react-ref-mcq-001",type:"multiple-choice",topic:"Refs",subtopic:"Refs vs State",difficulty:"medium",question:"A stopwatch needs to store its `setInterval` ID so a Stop button can clear it. Ref or state — and why?\n```js\nfunction handleStart() {\n  /* ??? */ = setInterval(() => setNow(Date.now()), 10);\n}\n```",options:["A ref — the ID isn't used for rendering, and updating it shouldn't cause a re-render","State — all values inside a component must live in state","A module-level variable outside the component","Either works identically"],answer:"A ref — the ID isn't used for rendering, and updating it shouldn't cause a re-render",explanation:`\`\`\`js
const intervalRef = useRef(null);

function handleStart() {
  intervalRef.current = setInterval(() => setNow(Date.now()), 10);
}
function handleStop() {
  clearInterval(intervalRef.current);
}
\`\`\`
The ID never appears in JSX — only event handlers need it. State would cause pointless re-renders; a module-level variable would be shared across all instances of the component. The guideline: state for what's on screen, refs for everything else (timeout IDs, DOM nodes, library objects).`,tags:["refs","state","mcq"]},{id:"react-ref-002",topic:"Refs",subtopic:"Refs vs State",difficulty:"medium",question:"Compare refs and state on: how you update them, whether updates re-render, and when you can read them.",answer:"State: update via setter, triggers re-render, readable any time (each render sees its snapshot). Refs: mutate `ref.current` directly, no re-render, and should *not* be read or written during rendering — only from event handlers and Effects.",explanation:"The two are complementary, not interchangeable. A counter shown on screen in a ref never updates visually (no re-render); an interval ID in state causes wasted renders. One blessed exception to the no-render-access rule: lazy first-render initialization like `if (!ref.current) ref.current = new Thing();`.",tags:["refs","state"]},{id:"react-ref-mcq-002",type:"multiple-choice",topic:"Refs",subtopic:"Pitfalls",difficulty:"hard",question:`Why is this Toggle broken?
\`\`\`js
function Toggle() {
  const isOnRef = useRef(false);
  return (
    <button onClick={() => { isOnRef.current = !isOnRef.current; }}>
      {isOnRef.current ? 'On' : 'Off'}
    </button>
  );
}
\`\`\``,options:["Clicking flips the ref but causes no re-render, so the label never updates — this value belongs in state","Refs cannot hold booleans, only DOM elements","The ref resets to false on every render","The onClick closure captures a stale ref object"],answer:"Clicking flips the ref but causes no re-render, so the label never updates — this value belongs in state",explanation:"`isOnRef.current` is read during render to choose the label, but mutating a ref doesn't tell React anything — the component never re-renders, so the screen shows a stale value forever. Anything used to compute JSX must be state:\n```js\nconst [isOn, setIsOn] = useState(false);\n```\nThe symptom (UI not updating despite the value changing) is the classic sign of rendering from a ref.",tags:["refs","pitfalls","mcq"]},{id:"react-ref-003",topic:"Refs",subtopic:"useRef Basics",difficulty:"medium",question:"What kinds of values are good candidates for refs?",answer:"Information that event handlers or external systems need but that never affects what's rendered: timeout/interval IDs, DOM elements, and instances of non-React objects (players, sockets, library handles).",explanation:"The litmus test from react.dev: if changing the value should change what's on screen, it's state; if the component only needs to *remember* it between renders without re-rendering, it's a ref. DOM access (`<div ref={myRef}>` then `myRef.current.focus()`) is the most common case and the subject of its own page.",tags:["refs","use-cases"]},{id:"react-ctx-004",topic:"Context",subtopic:"When to Use",difficulty:"medium",question:"Name the common use cases react.dev lists for context.",answer:"Theming (dark mode), the current user/account, routing (current route), and app-wide state management — often a reducer's `state` and `dispatch` provided together.",explanation:"```js\nconst [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, initialState);\n\n<StateContext value={{ state, dispatch }}>\n  {children}\n</StateContext>\n```\nThe common thread: data that many distant components need, where threading props would touch dozens of files. Context combined with `useReducer` is the built-in pattern that libraries like Redux formalize.",tags:["context","use-cases","useReducer"]}];export{e as default};
